Usage: aether [options] [command]

Aether - a cross-platform terminal AI coding agent. Building tools that break
assumptions.

Options:
  -v, --version                output the version number
  --no-tui                     force the classic readline REPL instead of the
                               interactive TUI
  -h, --help                   display help for command

Commands:
  ask [options] <question>     ask a one-shot question and stream the answer
  use <spec>                   set the default provider and/or model (e.g.
                               aether use zen/claude-sonnet-5)
  models [options] [provider]  list models for a provider (default provider if
                               omitted; zen fetches live)
  providers                    list all providers with key status
  swarm [options] <task>       decompose a task and run parallel subagents
  agent [options] <task>       route a task to a subagent profile and run it
  agents                       list agent profiles
  jobs [id]                    show background job status (list all when no id
                               is given)
  sessions                     manage saved chat sessions
  doctor                       run environment diagnostics (config, keys,
                               sessions)
  upgrade                      check npm registry for the latest version
  completions <shell>          print shell completion script (bash, zsh, fish)
  alias                        manage command aliases
  config                       view or edit the config file
  connect                      interactive provider setup — pick a provider and
                               paste your key (the opencode /connect pattern)
  login <provider>             save an API key for a provider (e.g. aether login
                               openai)
  logout <provider>            remove the saved API key for a provider
  keys                         show which API keys are configured and where
  login-device                 OAuth device flow sign-in (github, google) — the
                               opencode pattern
  logout-device                remove a device-flow sign-in (github, google)
  cache                        token-efficiency cache (repeated prompts cost 0
                               tokens)
  status                       show current configuration summary
  cost                         show approximate usage totals from saved sessions
  stats                        show aggregated token usage across all sessions
  mcp                          manage MCP servers
  sync                         sync sessions across devices (free)
  recall <query>               search every past session for a phrase
                               (cross-session memory)
